{
  "id": "avinu_malkeinu_mincha",
  "cardId": "mincha",
  "title": {
    "he": "אָבִֽינוּ מַלְכֵּֽנוּ",
    "en": "Avinu Malkeinu"
  },
  "source": "feigenbaum",
  "sourcePages": [
    179,
    183
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tags": [
        "rubric"
      ],
      "body": [
        {
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "On fast days (except Tishah B'Av) and during the Aseres Yemei Teshuvah, we add Avinu Malkeinu before Tachanun:"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "body": [
        {
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Is Hashem our kind and compassionate Father (אבינו) Who understands our failings, or is He our King (מלכנו) with demands and rules? The answer is—He is both! Hashem is our King, with a special mission for each of us, with rules and structures to help us maximize our spiritual potential to fulfill that mission. And we must never lose sight of our responsibilities and the end goal—those highest rungs of the ladder we climb in our avodas Hashem. Our decisions do have very serious consequences!"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "body": [
        {
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Yet, importantly, He is also our Father Who understands that at times we stumble, lose our footing, and get sidetracked from our goal. Through all this, He still supports us and gives us multiple opportunities to get back on track. On these special days, when we think about who we really are, what our mission is, what our real potential is, and take stock of our progress, we need to focus on both those aspects of our relationship with Him. By doing that, we will be worthy of Hashem giving us what we are asking for so that we can get back up and be the people He created us to be."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "instant_insight",
      "body": [
        {
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "NOTE: The Hebrew of this tefillah is quite simple and straightforward. I urge you to say each line slowly in the original."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
